Day 7: Challenges of Machine Learning

1. Data collection

If there is no data, there is no point in doing machine learning.

We get data easily in small projects like Kaggle.
But in a real company, data gathering is very hard.

We can fetch data from an API or do web scraping.

This is one of the main problems in ML.

2. Insufficient data / labeled data

If we have two algorithms: algorithm A is much better in theory, but A has 100 rows of data and B has 1 lakh rows. Even if A is the better algorithm, B will often perform better because it has more data.

If we have too much data, the choice of algorithm matters less.
We often only have small or medium data.

If we have insufficient data, ML is hard.

3. Non representative data

If the data covers only half the real world, the result will be only half useful.

Example: if we survey only Spain about who will win the World Cup, ML may say Spain will win, because most respondents pick Spain.

We need data that represents all countries playing in the World Cup.

Terms: sampling noise, sampling bias.

4. Poor quality data

Most of the time we spend cleaning data, often around 60% of the time.
The purer the data, the better the model.

5. Irrelevant features / columns

Some columns are never useful. Garbage in, garbage out.

Example: a survey asks who will win a marathon. We have age, height, weight, and location.
Age and weight matter. Location alone cannot tell us that people from Pokhara will win.
Height and weight can be combined into BMI instead of keeping both separately.

6. Overfitting

The model memorizes old training data. It answers well on old data but fails on new data.

Example: one cinema ticket in Kathmandu costs 120. The model generalizes and says every hall ticket everywhere is 120.

If the model tries to be too accurate on training data alone, that is overfitting.

7. Underfitting

The model is not accurate enough. It fails on training data and on real new data.

If accuracy on training data is pushed to 100%, that is often overfitting.
If accuracy is very low, that is underfitting.

8. Software integration

After we build ML, we must integrate it into software.
We must check that ML fits the product and stays compatible.

Software integration is very hard.
Always try to build an end to end product.

9. Offline learning / deployment

Once the model is online, data must keep updating.
We often retrain offline and push to the server again. That cycle is hard.

Deployment is very hard.
AWS and Azure help, but it is still not always stable.

Last challenge: cost involved

Cost can be very high when many people use the ML system.
If we host on our own server, hidden costs can grow fast.
Monetizing ML is very hard.

Main tip: always aim for an end to end product. While learning ML you also learn MLOps, cost optimization, load balancing, and more.
